    "source": "1790 and 1800 census interpolated to 1796, free adult men outside the Southern states; the 1790 census gives only free white males 16 and over, so men 21+ are estimated. Voters were men meeting each state's rules: most states required a freehold, property or taxpaying qualification (Vermont, Kentucky and, for taxpayers, Georgia and Pennsylvania were broader). Electors were chosen by popular vote in 8 states; elsewhere (Connecticut, Delaware, New Jersey, New York, Rhode Island, South Carolina and Vermont (Tennessee used electors named by county-chosen electors; Massachusetts's legislature chose from district candidates)) men cast no presidential vote. Free Black men could vote in some Northern states on the same or stricter terms."
   },
   {
    "key": "women",
