    "B": 0.0,
    "O": 0.0,
    "source": "1790 and 1800 census interpolated to 1796, Black adults in Virginia, North Carolina, South Carolina, Georgia and Tennessee; the census gives free people of colour without age or sex before 1820, so adults are estimated. The great majority were enslaved and could not vote. Free Black men meeting the property tests could legally vote only in North Carolina (until its 1835 constitution) and Tennessee (until its 1834 constitution); the other Southern states limited the vote to white men. In South Carolina the legislature chose the electors, so no one there voted for president directly."
   }
